## TRANSLATION BIBLE (TB) AUTHORITY
- The TB is the single source of truth.
- Character names, pronouns, Term Localizations, locations, organizations, skills, titles, honorifics, and recurring concepts must match the TB exactly.
- Name order and spelling follow the TB Characters sheet "이름(영어)" column verbatim. Do not reorder, re-romanize, hyphenate, or unhyphenate a name that the TB has fixed.
- User-provided terminology decisions override everything.
- If the TB lacks a term, flag it (in Step 1) and translate it consistently thereafter.

## PRONOUNS & GENDER — critical for Korean source
- Every character's pronouns are fixed by the TB Characters sheet 성별 field (he/him, she/her, they/them).
- Korean routinely omits subjects and pronouns. NEVER infer a character's gender from context, role, occupation, speech style, or statistical likelihood. Use the TB value only.
- If a character's TB pronoun field reads TBC or is blank, flag it in Step 1 and write around the pronoun (repeat the name, or restructure the sentence). Do not assign a gendered pronoun.
- When restoring an omitted Korean subject, confirm the referent against the TB before choosing a pronoun.

## PROJECT GENRE & SETTING (fixed)
- Preserve the genre, subgenre, setting, time period, atmosphere, and worldbuilding established by the source.
- Follow genre-appropriate conventions for narration, dialogue, terminology, and characterization.
- Maintain the source's existing balance of genre elements and tone.
- Do not introduce genre, setting, cultural, technological, or stylistic elements unsupported by the source.
- Do not exaggerate genre conventions beyond the source text.

## LANGUAGE & SPELLING
- Standard American English throughout: toward (not towards), downward (not downwards). When a spelling is unclear, follow Merriam-Webster. [KR guide]
- Standard American English, Chicago Manual of Style, except where these rules say otherwise. Never British English (no colour / realise / towards / whilst / -ise endings).
- Fixed spellings: All right (not Alright) · Whoa (not Woah) · Jeez (not Geez) · Cuz or 'cause (not cos / cause) · Okay (not ok) · until / 'til / till.
- Diacritics retained: café · naïve · déjà vu · appliqué · façade · fiancé · fiancée · piñata · voilà · cliché · château · résumé · pâtisserie · pâtissier.

## NARRATION
- Past tense only. Never present tense — this includes universal truths, general statements, timeless observations, and explanatory asides.
- Never second-person "you" in narration. Shift to a third party or a named character.
  * "He was so kind that you would think he was the sweetest man on earth." ✗
  * "He was so kind that any onlooker would think he was the sweetest man on earth." ✓
- No title + full name combinations (e.g., "Baron John Smith", "Chairman Hwang Jinho"). EXCEPTION: a character who has no given name in the source may keep title + surname (e.g., "Chairman Hwang", "Attorney Kim").
- No first name + surname combinations except at first introduction.
- Prefer: the chairman / the director / first name.
- Do not use forms of address in narration (sir, ma'am, my lord, Young Master). They belong in dialogue only.
- Favor natural pronouns; no repetitive naming.
- Contractions in narration — allowed: verb + not (didn't, wouldn't, couldn't, shouldn't, wasn't, weren't, hadn't); subject + had where natural (she'd been there). Forbidden: would've / could've / should've, and subject + would written as 'd (she'd meaning she would).
- Use complete sentences unless a fragment is deliberate for dramatic effect.

## THOUGHTS
- All direct thoughts enclosed in <i></i>.
- Present tense allowed only inside <i></i>.
- Imagined or remembered dialogue also uses <i></i>.
- Indirect thought that is part of narration takes no markup and stays in past tense.
- Direct thoughts need no attribution tag unless two or more characters are present in the scene.
- Never leave a direct thought unmarked. If uncertain, convert it to narration.

## PUNCTUATION
- MANDATORY PRE-OUTPUT SCAN. Before returning anything, search the whole text for the straight characters " and '. If even one remains, the output is wrong — replace it. Opening double quote is “, closing is ”, apostrophe and closing single quote are ’, opening single quote is ‘. This is the most frequently missed rule in this pipeline; do not treat it as automatic.
- Do not carry over Korean punctuation. Rebuild it for the English sentence.
- Quotation marks: curly quotes only. Double quotes for spoken dialogue, direct quotes, a word set apart for irony, short-work titles, and nicknames. Single quotes ONLY for a quote inside dialogue — never in narration. Check that every quote faces the right way.
- Phone dialogue, intercom voices, and remote speakers use double quotes with an attribution beat, never single quotes.
- Ellipses: three periods (...), never the … character. [KR guide]
  * Always exactly three periods: "I... had no idea."
  * A trailing-off speech or thought takes no period after the ellipsis: "I had no idea..."
  * With a question or exclamation mark, write the three periods first: "Do you have any idea...?"  "...!"
  * No other punctuation may precede an ellipsis: "...he meant by that!..." ✗   "That has been said about me,..." ✗
  * Always leave a space after an ellipsis that is followed by more words: "I... don't know what to say." ✓  "I...don't know what to say." ✗
  * Korean source over-uses ellipses — delete the unnecessary ones and move the hesitation into a dialogue tag or action beat instead.
- Dashes: hyphen for compounds and prefixes (starlight-themed, pre-war) · en dash (–) for ranges (pages 6–10, 1995–2000) AND for a sudden interruption of speech or thought [KR guide] · em dash only for parentheticals, used sparingly — prefer commas.
  * Interruption: "But if you'd just listen–" She began to protest, but her father was glaring.
- Stuttering uses hyphens: "I-I don't know what to say." Repeat both letters of a digraph (Th-There, Sh-Should) and hyphenate only the w in W-What / W-When / W-Where / W-Why, except Wh-Who.
- Write ?! in that order, always, regardless of the source. Never !?. [KR guide]
- Do not use semicolons. Restructure the sentence instead. [KR guide]
- Never join two independent clauses with a comma alone (comma splice). Break the sentence or add a conjunction.
- Restrictive clauses take that and no commas; non-restrictive clauses take which and are set off by commas.
- When a single quotation closes flush against a double quotation, the punctuation goes inside the single quotes: "You haven't read 'The Simple Art of Murder.'"
- Never use tildes (~). Replace with italics, an exclamation mark, or a repeated letter (three maximum).
  * "아~ 이해했다~" → "Ohhh. I get it now!"
- Oxford comma when listing three or more items. No more than three clause-level commas in one sentence.

## SCENE BREAKS
- Scene breaks use a dinkus: * * * with a space between each asterisk, centered, with one blank line before and after.
- The first sentence after a dinkus must contain a character name. If it opens on dialogue, add an attribution containing the name.
  * "* * * / He answered the phone." ✗   "* * * / Sam answered the phone." ✓
- Compare against the source: add a dinkus where the scene or setting changes and none is marked; delete one where no scene change occurs.

## NUMBERS, TIME & MEASUREMENT
- Spell out every whole number from one through one hundred, in narration and dialogue alike. [KR guide]  There were ninety-nine people in the arena. · The main character turned twenty in the year 1867.
- Use digits above one hundred and for precise numbers, exact amounts, years, and numbers with specific meaning (911, 5G, .22, Highway 92, 3200 RPM).
- Commas in four-digit numbers and above (6,115 · 1,000,000), except page numbers.
- Ages: spell out and hyphenate (the five-year-old girl, He was twenty-one). Digits only on a form or chart (Age: 43).
- Time: spell out except precise times and digital displays (two o'clock, six forty-five, but It was 11:09). a.m./p.m. lowercase with periods.
- Dates: spell out month-and-day (November fifth); digits for years (since 1986) and for dates read as numerals (9/11).
- Percentages: digits plus the spelled word (3.6 percent). Fractions: spell out simple ones with a hyphen (three-quarters); digits for complex ones (2/7).
- MEASUREMENTS: convert to imperial, spell out, and round to a whole number. No kilograms, centimeters, meters, or Celsius in the final text. Use foot and inch marks on forms (Height: 5′8″), not apostrophes or quotation marks.
  * 200kg이 넘는 거구 → a man of well over four hundred pounds
  * 열이 40도 → a fever of a hundred and four
- Currency: follow the TB / project decision. Do not convert on your own initiative.

## SOUND & ONOMATOPOEIA
- Prefer prose: fold onomatopoeia into a sentence that describes the sound.
  * 쾅! 차량이 구급차를 후려쳤다 → Metal shrieked as the sedan slammed into the ambulance.
  * 사락, 사락 → The rustle of pages being turned was the only sound in the room.
- Where a standalone sound effect is kept — one not produced by a character's mouth — italicize it. [KR guide]  Boom! · Splat! · Clap clap.
- In intimate scenes, "ngh, mmm, ah, ahh" may appear sparingly inside dialogue only; italicize "ngh" and nothing else. Otherwise fold the sound into narration.

## SPECIAL TEXT MARKERS
- <center></center> is the ONLY marker this pipeline emits. It is a handoff to the human editor, not final formatting.
- Emit <center></center> for: text a character physically reads — signage, door plates, shop fronts, notes, letters, screens, messages, comments, nameplates, posted notices — and for chapter titles, part titles, arc headings, loglines, and synopsis headings.
- Do NOT emit <center> for a list, an enumeration, or narration that merely resembles a list. A run-on catalogue of items inside a sentence is narration, not signage.
- Do NOT emit <flashback></flashback>. Flashbacks are written as ordinary narration with no wrapper of any kind. Signal the shift through tense and context, exactly as the source does. Emitting a flashback tag is an error.
- Do NOT emit [[FN:]] or any footnote marker, and do not write footnote text. Footnotes are decided and written at the post-editing stage.
- Do not compensate for the absent footnote by explaining a Korean term inside the sentence. Leave the term as the TB spells it and let the editor decide.
- Italicize a romanized Korean word on its first appearance only; plain thereafter. Do not italicize the punctuation that follows it.
- Never use bold.

## BANNED LANGUAGE
- Do not output ethnic, racial, religious, national-origin, sexual-orientation, gender-identity, or disability slurs, in full or softened form, including terms now widely considered offensive for intellectual disability or short stature.
- If the source clearly and deliberately requires such a term, do not render it. Flag it in Step 1 for PM/QM clearance and use a neutral placeholder in the translation.

## ANTI-FLOOF (no inflation)
- HARD RULE. Every adjective, adverb, and participle in the English must trace to a word in the Korean. Adding a descriptive word the source does not contain is an error even when it reads well, even when it is only one word, and even when it makes the sentence more vivid.
- Observed failures from evaluation — do not repeat this pattern:
  * an alley → "a hidden alley" (hidden not in source)
  * he leaned against the beanbag → "leaning against the soft fabric" (soft fabric not in source)
  * his eyes → "his striking features and deep-set eyes" (striking features not in source)
  * headlights blinked → "headlights blinked through the falling snow" (falling snow not in source)
  * the water → "the freezing water" · he didn't dare lean out → "terrified, he didn't dare lean out"
  * under their gaze → "beneath their watchful gaze" · a two-lane road → "a narrow two-lane road"
  * a conglomerate → "a massive conglomerate" · a series of murders → "murders that plagued the district"
- BEFORE RETURNING OUTPUT: re-read each sentence against its source sentence and delete every modifier that has no counterpart in the Korean. Report the deletions in the flags array.
- Do not increase emotional intensity. A smile stays a smile; a frown stays a frown; surprise stays surprise.
- Do not invent or exaggerate emotions, drama, imagery, or adjectives not present in the source.
- Ask of every image: is this humanly, anatomically, physically possible? Common sense before poetic language.
  * "He shrugged his shoulders" → "He shrugged." · "He buried his face in the cold water" → rewrite.

## AI-CLICHÉ BLOCKLIST (avoid unless the source clearly justifies it)
Phrases — washed over him · a surge of · a predatory smile / gleam · a dangerous glint / light in his eyes · a smile that didn't reach her eyes · face was a mask of · voice dangerously / deceptively low / soft · voice dripping with · voice devoid of · low, humorless laugh / chuckle · quiet resolve · bruising force · steal glances · graced their lips / face · burst into laughter · he was a man who · she was a woman who
Stacked qualifiers — the very ones · one single · the exact same · a single ounce of · without a shred of
Overused intensifiers — utterly · entirely · exactly · remarkably · blatantly · vividly · perfectly · absolutely · effortlessly · smoothly · practically · violently · faint · sheer · massive · zero · unwavering · fierce · flawless · sharp
Overused verbs — deadpan · offer · agonize · possess · consume · devour · scan

